Transaction - sequence of operations performed using one or more SQL statements as a single
logical unit of work

ACID transaction
Atomic - all changes must be performed successfully or not at all
Consistent - data must be in a consistent state before and after the transaction
Isolated - no other process can change the data while the transaction is running
Durable - the changes made by the transaction must persist

Pseudocode for a banking system: Transferring money from one account to another
1. Start Transaction
2. Check if the source account has enough funds
    If not enough funds, ABORT transaction
3. Deduct the specified amount from the source account
4. Add the specified amount to the destination account
5. Check if the total balance before and after the transaction is the same
    If not consistent, ROLLBACK the changes
6. Commit the transaction if all steps are successful
7. End Transaction

Transaction Control Language
BEGIN - start the ACID transaction
COMMIT - all statements complete successfully, save the new database state (updating each data entries)
ROLLBACK - one or more statements fail, undo changes
BEGIN TRANSACTION;

-- Assuming we have two accounts in the 'accounts' table, identified by their 'account_id'
DECLARE @sourceAccountID INT = 1;
DECLARE @destinationAccountID INT = 2;
DECLARE @transferAmount DECIMAL(10, 2) = 100.00;

-- Check if the source account has enough balance
IF (SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE account_id = @sourceAccountID) >= @transferAmount
BEGIN
    -- Deduct the amount from the source account
    UPDATE accounts
    SET balance = balance - @transferAmount
    WHERE account_id = @sourceAccountID;

    -- Add the amount to the destination account
    UPDATE accounts
    SET balance = balance + @transferAmount
    WHERE account_id = @destinationAccountID;

    -- Commit the transaction
    COMMIT TRANSACTION;
END
ELSE
BEGIN
    -- Not enough funds, rollback the transaction
    ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;
END